{
  "title": "The Rise of Urban Beekeeping",
  "paragraphs": [
    "In recent years, beekeeping has moved from rural farms to city rooftops and balconies. Urban beekeeping clubs have sprung up in many major cities, and local governments have started to view these hobbyists as partners in promoting biodiversity. Supporters argue that bees thrive in cities because of the wide variety of flowers in parks and gardens, and because cities are often warmer than surrounding countryside, extending the foraging season.",
    "However, some ecologists warn that the growing number of urban hives may not be good news for all pollinators. Honeybees are generalists and can outcompete wild bees for limited floral resources. In dense city centers, where green spaces are fragmented, this competition can be intense. A study in Paris found that wild bee diversity declined in areas with high hive density, suggesting that adding more honeybee colonies does not automatically enhance overall pollinator health.",
    "Despite these concerns, many urban beekeepers remain enthusiastic. They point to the educational value of their hobby and the social connections it fosters. Some cities now require beekeepers to register their hives and to follow best practices, such as planting pollinator-friendly gardens and avoiding overcrowding. These measures aim to balance the benefits of urban beekeeping with the need to protect wild pollinators."
  ],
  "items": [
    {
      "prompt": "What reason do supporters give for bees doing well in cities?",
      "answers": ["B"],
      "skill": "detail",
      "evidence": "Supporters argue that bees thrive in cities because of the wide variety of flowers in parks and gardens, and because cities are often warmer than surrounding countryside, extending the foraging season.",
      "explanation": "The text explicitly states that supporters believe bees thrive in cities due to the variety of flowers and warmer temperatures. Choice B accurately reflects this.",
      "options": {
        "A": "Cities have fewer predators than rural areas.",
        "B": "Cities offer diverse flowers and a longer foraging season due to warmth.",
        "C": "Urban beekeepers provide bees with artificial food.",
        "D": "Cities have cleaner air than the countryside."
      },
      "distractors": {
        "A": "The text does not mention predators as a reason for urban bee success.",
        "C": "Artificial feeding is not mentioned in the passage.",
        "D": "Air quality is not discussed as a factor."
      }
    },
    {
      "prompt": "The word 'fragmented' in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to:",
      "answers": ["C"],
      "skill": "meaningInContext",
      "evidence": "In dense city centers, where green spaces are fragmented, this competition can be intense.",
      "explanation": "In this context, 'fragmented' means broken up into small, disconnected pieces, which makes green spaces scattered and limited. Choice C best captures this meaning.",
      "options": {
        "A": "abundant",
        "B": "connected",
        "C": "broken up",
        "D": "protected"
      },
      "distractors": {
        "A": "Abundant is the opposite of fragmented in this context.",
        "B": "Connected is the opposite of fragmented.",
        "D": "Protected does not fit the context of green spaces being scattered."
      }
    },
    {
      "prompt": "What can be inferred about the study in Paris?",
